  "answer_text": "Here is a helpful guide on medicines you can take when you get a cold. Please note that this is general information and **not a substitute for professional medical advice** \u2014 always consult your doctor or pharmacist for personalized recommendations.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udd27 Managing the Common Cold: Medicine Options\n\n### Important First Note\nThe common cold has no cure but should improve on its own. Antibiotics don't work against viruses and won't help you feel better. Because there's no cure for the common cold, the best thing you can do is ease the symptoms. Over-the-counter (OTC) drugs can help with many different symptoms.\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udc8a Pain Relievers & Fever Reducers\nAcetaminophen (Tylenol) and ibuprofen (Advil, Motrin) help lower fever and relieve muscle aches. Painkillers like ibuprofen and acetaminophen can also relieve cold-related symptoms such as sore throat, headache, and earache.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Aspirin can treat a cold, but it may have potential serious side effects, such as stomach bleeding, so it's best to avoid using it. Aspirin should **never** be used in children and teens due to the risk of Reye syndrome.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83d\udc43 Nasal Decongestants\nNasal decongestants help unclog a congested nose. They work by narrowing the blood vessels in the lining of your nose so that the swollen tissue shrinks and decreases mucous production.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f Antihistamines\nOver-the-counter antihistamines, such as chlorpheniramine and diphenhydramine, may relieve cold symptoms for one or two days. A combination of antihistamines and decongestants may work better.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Antihistamines such as diphenhydramine or doxylamine can cause drowsiness, so they're best for nighttime use.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83e\udd27 Cough Suppressants\nThe most common OTC cough suppressant is dextromethorphan.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udca7 Expectorants\nExpectorants help thin and loosen mucus, making it easier to cough up when you have a wet cough. Guaifenesin is a common ingredient.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83e\uddf4 Combination Cold Medicines\nSome medicines mix ingredients such as antihistamines, decongestants, and/or pain relievers with cough medicine. Check whether your OTC cold medicine has an AM/daytime or PM/nighttime version. The daytime medicine might not make you sleepy, but it won't necessarily help keep you awake.\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaway\nThe drug that you choose will depend on your specific symptoms. Ask your healthcare provider or pharmacist about over-the-counter (OTC) medicines, such as pain relievers and fever reducers. These can lessen symptoms and help you feel better.\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Disclaimer:** This information is for general guidance only. Always consult a qualified healthcare professional before starting any medication, especially if you have underlying conditions or are taking other medicines.",
